r the adversarial file-shape table, the trailing-comment decoy included -> after: every enforcer reads the same value through ONE parse; the hooks' and checkers' answers agree over that table, decoy included; and an unreadable declaration refuses at the hooks rather than reading a default. The Coincident restates the same three conditions as the need's pinned equality -> not the same: an implementation with two agreeing grammars passed the old text and fails \"through one parse\", and the unreadable-refusal threshold is new. Ready to bless. The criterion is an observable condition with a pass line, in capability voice, naming artifact classes (hooks, checkers) and no file. The unreadable arm is the honest fit criterion of the Requirement's \"one parse, one answer\": a file with no answer must not be given a default by any reader. That is the fail-open class SECURITY, already on the row's Hat-Refs, exists for, and the spec's delegated decision D-006 rules it. The Coincident still describes the need as read by \"two grammars\". That is accurate about SN-028's own text, which the scope split leaves to the shape-rule retirement row.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-031 Detail -> before: the single first-non-comment-line reader that the hooks, the loop's declared reader and the privacy checker all use -> after: the same sentence, plus: one parse of the policy file per run, read by every checker gate; an unreadable or unparseable file is a distinct outcome, never a default; only the exact opt-down value under the policies table opts the unwired reviewer down, while a copy elsewhere or an unreadable file requires the reviewer, and the legacy record answers only when the key is absent; and the hooks receive one closed five-way answer from that parse (as IF-298 carries) and no longer read a declared line themselves -> not the same: the hooks' text read is gone, and a new posture rule and a new unreadable outcome appear. Ready to bless. The added clauses are refactor-proof (inputs, outcome, where it refuses), add no implementation name beyond those the approved sentence already carried, and cite the interface by id rather than restating its tokens. The kept opening sentence (\"the hooks ... all use\") reads awkwardly beside \"they no longer read a declared line themselves\". It is reconciled by \"reach it through that answer\", meaning the hooks use the reader through the probe's call. A future tidy may fold the two, but the obligation is unambiguous.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-021 Detail and parent (sr_refs gains SR-031) -> before: the one 3.11+ interpreter probe for the three hooks, with its candidate order, its refusal naming the install step, and its candidates matching developer setup -> after: the same, plus: after the interpreter check, one scripts-directory resolution for all three hooks (an override naming no directory refuses with no fallback; otherwise the default directory in either letter case; none found refuses); then one parse of the policy, once per hook run, for a closed answer, and the hook acts only on an exact answer, refusing a mixed or unreadable declaration, a failed read or any other answer, naming the cause -> not the same: two hooks that fell back on a bad override now refuse, and the policy read and its ordering after the probe are new obligations. Ready to bless. The parent change is earned: the new policy-read sentences carry SR-031's one-parse read on the consumer side, while the scripts-directory clause sits under the hook-refusal parents SR-019 and SR-020 it already had. LLR-031 owns what the parse answers and LLR-021 owns when the hooks ask and how they act on it, so the two rows are one decision each. Two minor notes, neither a reason to return the row. First, LLR-021 lists the five answers in prose where LLR-031 defers to IF-298; a link would leave the set one home. Second, of the new resolution arms, TC-021 drives only the bad-override refusal. The none-found refusal with no override is driven at pre-push by `test_pre_push_honors_kit_scripts_dir`, outside TC-021's evidence, and it reaches the one shared resolution all three hooks call. The upper-case fallback is carried-over behaviour with no driven case, and its regression would fail closed.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-021 Expected and Method -> before: drive the three hooks with non-runnable and pre-3.11 interpreters, and pre-commit on a real virtual environment; inspect the shared probe and its candidate list -> after: the same, plus: drive each hook with a scripts override naming a missing directory, with an unreadable or mixed declaration, and with a policy answer that fails or falls outside its set; inspect that each hook makes the one read once, after the probe. Expected adds the matching refusals and the read-once-after-probe property -> not the same: new arms enter the verified set. Ready to bless. Each arm is observable and matches a Done-when bullet (the bad-override refusal, the unreadable and mixed refusals, the failed or foreign answer, once after the probe) and an LLR-021 clause. Every cited node exists by name in the evidence cell. The resolution sub-arm noted under LLR-021 is the one place this TC could be wider; it does not make what the TC states false.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-031 Method -> before: run the process-config and policy suites (one value per dial, the hooks' sh grammar and tomllib agreeing over the shape table, a mixed config refused) -> after: drive each shipped commit and push hook over the adversarial table and compare its answer with the checker's for every shape; drive an unreadable and a mixed declaration through each hook; run the policy suites; exercise the unwired-reviewer posture (only the exact opt-down value under the policies table warns, a copy requires review, the legacy record answers only when the key is absent); inspect that no hook reads a declared line itself -> not the same: the comparison moves from an extracted sh grammar to the real hooks, and the posture and no-text-read arms are new. Ready to bless. It verifies SR-031's amended criterion (agreement through the real hooks, the unreadable refusal) and LLR-031's new clauses (posture, hooks reach the parse only through the answer), and the posture and no-text-read nodes it names are cited in its evidence. It overlaps TC-021 on the unreadable and mixed drives, but each TC verifies a different row's claim: SR-031's criterion here, LLR-021's consumer refusal there.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=5\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":2,"oldLines":7,"newStart":2,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," ## amendment"," ","-All six rows changed MEANING against their anchors (SR-031 at 08bbb71b6; LLR-021, LLR-031, TC-021 and TC-031 at ed84e2510). Each now carries refusals, a posture rule, or a single-read obligation the blessed text did not. I would bless all six as they stand, so the later act turn may re-attest each.","+All five rows changed MEANING against their anchors (SR-031 at 08bbb71b6; LLR-021, LLR-031, TC-021 and TC-031 at ed84e2510). Each now carries refusals, a posture rule, or a single-read obligation the blessed text did not. I would bless all five as they stand, so the later act turn may re-attest each."," "," I read each row against its chain and against the WI-962 spec's Trust ruling and Done-when, and I checked the TC arms against the cited tests. The coordinator's out-of-scope facts (SR-137 and SN-028 still stating the two-grammar premise) are respected: neither row is judged here."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019hhWQZ8wRcjQWpCsJWfmPb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
